<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Birchas HaHodaah [The Blessing of Thanksgiving] — Halachah 5<br>Part 1 (Intro + §§ 1–8 / Oaseyos א–ח)<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">COMPLETE · Based on <span class="lr">LM I:31</span> (<i>Ais Lan Bira B'Davra</i>)</span></div>

<div class="sa"><b>Shulchan Aruch text:</b> Four must give thanks: sea-travelers, wilderness-travelers, etc., a sick person who was healed, and one who was imprisoned and released. The mnemonic: <i>"and all who live [v'chol ha'<b>ch</b>a'<b>y</b>'<b>i</b>'<b>m</b>'] shall thank You"</i> etc.</div>

<div class="sn">Introduction: Letters, Vowel-Points, Longings, and the Four <i>Tikunim</i></div>

<div class="p">Based on the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">Ais lan bira b'davra</span>" (<span class="lr">LM I:31</span>); see there. The principle is: <span class="h">tzedakah</span> governs all the celestial spheres, etc. All the sufferings a person has on his journeys are due to the celestial spheres, etc. — for there are stars that decree there shall be a road, etc., and stars that decree there shall be a wilderness, etc. Therefore the remedy for this is to give <span class="h">tzedakah</span> before going on a journey, as it says: <span class="v">"righteousness shall go before him and shall direct his steps on the way"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="p">The essential completeness of the spheres — the completeness of <span class="h">tzedakah</span> — is only through <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, etc., which is the aspect of <span class="h">emunah</span>. The essential sustaining of <span class="h">emunah</span> is through the aspect of <span class="h">bris</span>, etc. There are two aspects of <span class="h">bris</span>: the aspect of Avraham, which is <span class="h">bris ilah</span> [the upper covenant] — the firmament that separates between the upper waters and the lower waters; and the aspect of Eliezer, which is <span class="h">bris tata'ah</span> [the lower covenant] — the aspect of Chanoch-<span class="h">Metat</span>, etc. This is the firmament that separates between clean waters and impure waters, between forbidden and permitted, etc. <span class="h">Bris ilah</span> is guarding the holy <span class="h">bris</span>. <span class="h">Bris tata'ah</span> is guarding forbidden and permitted, etc. A person must have both aspects of <span class="h">bris</span> — he must be both a <span class="h">tzadik</span> and a <span class="h">lamdan</span> [Torah scholar], etc.</div>

<div class="p">Through this one merits the aspect of <span class="h">chesed</span> — love and yearning for Hashem — and then he makes souls: he makes vowel-points for the letters of the Torah. For letters without vowel-points are like a body without a soul — they have no movement or function without a soul. So too, the combining and pairing of the letters through the 231 Gates to produce some action — they have no power except according to the vowel-points. The vowel-points are the love and longings, in the aspect of <span class="h">nekudos hakesef</span> [points of silver = points of longing]. The longings are the soul, etc. According to the longings, so are the vowel-points: if one longs for evil, he makes evil vowel-points, and then the letters combine and pair to produce evil actions. If one longs to return in <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, good vowel-points are made — good souls — and the letters move and pair to produce good actions, etc.</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="mx">"if the teacher resembles an angel of Hashem Tz'vakos"</span> — he must be both a <span class="h">tzadik</span> and a <span class="h">lamdan</span>, etc.; see all this there well.</div>

<div class="p">See what pertains to this, printed nearby, in my [Reb Noson's] own words. The principle is: the longings and yearning for something holy are exceedingly precious. Through them a good soul is formed and completed through speech, and it goes forth and transmigrates. Sometimes the soul of holiness transmigrates into a wicked person and thoughts of <span class="h">teshuvah</span> fall upon him, and he can be brought back to good. And conversely, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, how much evil is caused by longing for something evil, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — the soul formed through evil longings sometimes transmigrates into a <span class="h">tzadik</span> and can cause him to sin, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. This is: <span class="v">"there is a vanity that is done on the earth"</span> etc.; see all this there well.</div>

<div class="p">See there regarding the four who entered the <span class="h">Pardais</span> [orchard], etc. He explains there that one who separates a <span class="h">lamdan</span> from a <span class="h">tzadik</span> — who says that being a <span class="h">lamdan</span> alone is a virtue — is in the aspect of <span class="h">Acher</span> who "cut the plantings," who separated the aspect of <span class="h">Metat</span>, the aspect of an angel — the aspect of a <span class="h">lamdan</span>, the aspect of <span class="h">kasher</span> and <span class="h">pasul</span> [permitted and forbidden] — from <span class="v">"Hashem Tz'vakos"</span> — the aspect of a <span class="h">tzadik</span> with good deeds. For in truth, a <span class="h">lamdan</span> — even in Torah — without good deeds is nothing. On the contrary: <span class="mx">"if he did not merit, it becomes a potion of death for him."</span> Therefore the teacher must resemble an angel of <span class="v">"Hashem Tz'vakos"</span> — he is a <span class="h">lamdan</span> in Torah and serves Hashem with righteousness and good deeds. Through this he forms the letters of the Torah for good — then <span class="v">"Torah shall be sought from his mouth"</span>: the letters of the Torah seek to receive vowel-points and be formed from his mouth. Through his mouth speaking his holy yearnings and longings, the souls (= vowel-points for the letters) go forth, and the letters are formed and paired for good, etc. For the letters of the Torah are active agents — they are the vitality of everything and govern the world. Everything depends on the vowel-points that people draw through their longings for good or the reverse, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — through which the letters are formed for good or the reverse. Therefore Torah has two powers: <span class="mx">"if he merited, it becomes a potion of life"</span> etc.; see all this there well.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 1 — The Four Who Give Thanks: Crying Out to Hashem to Overpower the Evil Vapors</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the four who must give thanks for the miracle done for them — that they were in distress and were saved: those who wandered in the wilderness, etc. For all the troubles, salvations, and miracles that befall a person throughout his life — everything is according to how he merits forming the letters of the Torah for good, or the reverse, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, according to the longings, as above. Through this the entire world is governed for good, or the reverse, as explained there in the above <span class="h">Torah</span> well.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> is crying out to Hashem, as written there in Mizmor "<span class="h">Hodu</span>" which discusses all the above four — where it says regarding each one: <span class="v">"they cried out to Hashem"</span> etc. For the essential distress is because the evil vapors have overpowered — formed from evil longings, from which all troubles come, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, by the letters that govern the world being formed for ill, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, as above. Therefore the remedy is only: <span class="v">"they cried out to Hashem."</span> As our Sages say: <span class="mx">"crying out is good for a person"</span> etc. One must pray and cry out to Hashem so the holy vapors overpower and become souls — good vowel-points — to form the letters for good. Through this he is saved from the distress, as above.</div>

<div class="p">For the crying out is the aspect of a strong voice and vapor. Since he is in distress, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — indicating the evil vapors have already overpowered the good vapors — mere prayer in a simple voice is not enough. He must cry out in a great, strong voice to Hashem in order to overpower the holy vapor over the evil vapors, so that his affairs return to good, and through this he emerges from the distress. In the aspect of: <span class="v">"they cried out to Hashem in their distress, and from their afflictions He saved them,"</span> as above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore, when one emerges from distress, he must thank Hashem, as it says: <span class="v">"Let them give thanks to Hashem for His kindness"</span> etc. For the thanksgiving and praise to Hashem — this is the essential cleaving to Hashem, the aspect of will and longing and yearning for Hashem. All the thanksgivings, songs, and praises are included in the ten types of melody, which are all the aspect of a wondrous will and yearning — the essential cleaving to Hashem. This is: <span class="v">"let them give thanks to Hashem for His kindness"</span> — <span class="v">"His kindness"</span> specifically. And the <span class="h">mizmor</span> begins: <span class="v">"Give thanks to Hashem for He is good, for His kindness endures forever."</span> And it concludes: <span class="v">"whoever is wise... let them consider the kindnesses of Hashem"</span> — <span class="v">"kindnesses"</span> specifically: the aspect of <span class="h">chesed</span> explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>, which is the aspect of Avraham — wondrous will, love, and yearning for Hashem. This is: <span class="v">"let them give thanks to Hashem for His kindness and His wonders to the children of men"</span> — through this <span class="h">chesed</span> (= good will and longings), all the wonders in the world are made, by the letters being formed for good, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 2 — The Four Correspond to the Four <i>Tikunim</i></div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the four who must give thanks: those who wandered in the wilderness on a desolate path, etc.; those sitting in darkness — in prison — when they are released; the sick who are healed; and sea-travelers when they come up from there. All the dangers these four undergo correspond to the damage done in the aspects explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>, through which one attains good longings that rectify everything, as above.</div>

<div class="p">As explained there: <span class="h">tzedakah</span> governs all the celestial spheres, etc. The essential completeness of <span class="h">tzedakah</span> is through <span class="h">Shabbos</span>-<span class="h">emunah</span>. To merit this is through <span class="h">tikun habris</span>. <span class="h">Tikun habris</span> has two aspects: <span class="h">tzadik</span> and <span class="h">lamdan</span>, etc.; see there. This is the aspect of the four:</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"They wandered in the wilderness on a desolate path"</span> — this derives from damaging <span class="h">tzedakah</span>, which governs the celestial spheres, as explained there (ois daled). In his words: "Know that the sufferings a person has on journeys are due to the celestial spheres" etc. This is the aspect of: <span class="v">"righteousness shall go before him and shall direct his steps on the way"</span>; see there. The <span class="h">tikun</span> of the journey is through <span class="h">tzedakah</span>; see there.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Those sitting in darkness"</span> — in prison — derives from damaging <span class="h">Shabbos</span>-<span class="h">emunah</span>. For <span class="h">Shabbos</span> is freedom — the opposite of being imprisoned. See further below.</div>

<div class="p">The sick person who was healed — corresponds to the damage and <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">bris tata'ah</span>: forbidden and permitted, the firmament separating clean waters from impure waters, between forbidden and permitted, as explained there. This is the aspect of the distinction and sorting between blood and blood — between impure blood and pure blood, between bitter waters and sweet waters. According to the damage done in this, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — when one does not properly separate and sort between blood and blood, between pure and impure — all illnesses come, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. All illnesses come through the overpowering of murky blood through the damage of the four <span class="h">maros</span> [humors], which are the aspect of bitter waters — when one does not merit separating and distinguishing pure blood from impure blood, through sorting the <span class="h">halachah</span> to sort the forbidden from the permitted, the impure from the pure, etc. Thus the sick person who is healed corresponds to the damage and <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">bris tata'ah</span>, as above.</div>

<div class="p">Sea-travelers who come up — this corresponds to the <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">bris ilah</span>: the firmament separating the upper waters from the lower waters: <span class="v">"let there be a firmament in the midst of the waters, separating water from water."</span> This is the aspect of: <span class="v">"who makes a way in the sea and a path in the mighty waters"</span> — from which the power of all ships traveling in the sea is drawn, the aspect of <span class="v">"the way of a ship in the heart of the sea."</span> Therefore all who go down to the sea in ships, doing work in great waters — the essential power to rise from there in peace is through the power of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, who are in the aspect of <span class="h">tikun bris ilah</span>, the firmament separating water from water, from which the power of a way in the sea is drawn, the aspect of <span class="v">"who makes a way in the sea,"</span> the aspect of <span class="v">"the way of a ship in the heart of the sea,"</span> as above.</div>

<div class="p">Thus these four who must give thanks correspond to the four above-mentioned <span class="h">tikunim</span>: <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">tzedakah</span>; <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">Shabbos</span>-<span class="h">emunah</span>; <span class="h">tikun bris tata'ah</span> (= forbidden and permitted); and <span class="h">tikun bris ilah</span> (= guarding the holy <span class="h">bris</span> — the essential aspect of <span class="h">tzadik</span>). Through the <span class="h">tikun</span> of all four aspects, one merits good longings — good souls — through which the letters of the Torah are formed for good, through which one is saved from all troubles and dangers, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 3 — PaRDeS: The Four Parts of Torah Correspond to the Four</div>

<div class="p">For the essential purpose of Torah is when one merits good deeds: <span class="mx">"the purpose of Torah is teshuvah and good deeds"</span> etc. Torah governs the entire world. The essential thing is how the letters of Torah are formed — through good longings, as above. Through good deeds merited through good longings, the letters are formed for good and all four parts of Torah are rectified. These are the aspect of the above-mentioned four who must give thanks — the aspect of the four above-mentioned <span class="h">tikunim</span> explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>: <span class="h">tzedakah</span>, <span class="h">emunah</span>, etc.</div>

<div class="p">The four parts of Torah are the aspect of <span class="h">PaRDeS</span>: <span class="h">P'shat</span>, <span class="h">Remez</span>, <span class="h">Drush</span>, <span class="h">Sod</span>, as is known. None of them have completeness except through good longings, through which the essential formation for good is accomplished, as above. This is the aspect of the four who entered the <span class="h">Pardais</span> mentioned in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>: even though they merited entering the <span class="h">PaRDeS</span>, nevertheless they did not all emerge in peace in ultimate completeness — each one according to his longings and good deeds. Rabbi Akiva, who was complete in his good deeds with completeness — he alone entered in peace and exited in peace. Ben Azzai and Ben Zoma, even though they were great <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, were not completely perfect like Rabbi Akiva — therefore one "gazed and was stricken," etc. And <span class="h">Acher</span> — who had a stain in his heart, as proven by his end, when he seized a harlot in the marketplace, as our Sages say — therefore he "cut the plantings" entirely. Even though he was a great and outstanding <span class="h">lamdan</span> in Torah to such an extent, having all four parts of <span class="h">PaRDeS</span> in his hand — since he was not complete in his deeds as befitting, and his longings and desires were for evil — he found his [doom], until he became an apostate and "cut the plantings," <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>. The essential completeness of the four parts of <span class="h">PaRDeS</span> is through good longings. A <span class="h">lamdan</span> without righteousness is nothing.</div>

<div class="p">The four parts of <span class="h">PaRDeS</span> correspond to the above-mentioned four <span class="h">tikunim</span>, which are the aspect of the four who must give thanks: <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">tzedakah</span> (= journey-travelers), <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">Shabbos</span>-<span class="h">emunah</span> (= the imprisoned who are released), <span class="h">tikun</span> of forbidden and permitted (= the sick who are healed), and <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">bris kodesh</span> (= sea-travelers who rise from there in peace).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 4 — <i>P'shat</i> = <i>Bris Tata'ah</i> = Healing the Sick</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">P'shat</span> [the plain meaning] is the aspect of the <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">bris tata'ah</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">Metat</span>, who encompasses the six orders of Mishnah, who sorts the forbidden and permitted, etc., as above. All this is the aspect of <span class="h">P'shat</span> — the plain <span class="h">halachah</span>, through which the essential sorting of <span class="h">halachah</span> is accomplished, to sort the forbidden from the permitted, etc. All this is the aspect of <span class="h">tikun bris tata'ah</span>, as above. One who damages this — through this illness comes, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>, by not sorting between blood and blood, between clean waters and impure waters, etc.</div>

<div class="p">All this is the aspect of the damage of <span class="h">P'shat</span> — not sorting the plain <span class="h">halachah</span>. This is the aspect of: <span class="v">"they embittered their lives with hard labor"</span> — <span class="mx">"with difficulty"</span>: difficulties in the <span class="h">halachah</span>, etc., as brought in the Tikunim. He finds questions and difficulties and cannot clarify and sort it out — and through this <span class="v">"they embittered their lives."</span> This is the aspect of illness that comes through the overpowering of the four humors, the aspect of bitter waters — from which all illnesses come, embittering a person's life, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. Illness is the corruption of life, the aspect of <span class="v">"they embittered their lives"</span> etc. Illness is close to death, as it says: <span class="v">"they reached the gates of death."</span> This derives from damaging the aspect of <span class="h">P'shat</span>, which is the damage of <span class="h">bris tata'ah</span> — not meriting sorting the <span class="h">halachah</span> to sort the forbidden and permitted, to separate clean waters from impure waters, blood from blood — all due to the difficulty and heaviness of the <span class="h">halachah</span>, the aspect of <span class="v">"they embittered their lives with hard labor — with difficulty"</span> etc. Thus <span class="h">P'shat</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">bris tata'ah</span>, whose damage brings illness, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. Conversely, according to the <span class="h">tikun</span>, one merits healing — the aspect of the sick person who was healed, one of the above-mentioned four.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 5 — <i>Remez</i> = <i>Tzedakah</i> = Guiding the Way</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Remez</span> [allusion] — this is the aspect of the <span class="h">tikun</span> of journey-travelers, which is through <span class="h">tzedakah</span>, as above. For the essential knowledge of the straight path in absolute truth is impossible to indicate explicitly — only through allusion, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"I shall enlighten you and guide you on this path you shall walk; I shall advise — My eye is upon you."</span> It is impossible to explain and specify all the confusions and errors in the walking of paths, even physically — for from every path, many confusing paths branch off to the sides where one can go astray. How much more so regarding spiritual paths in the service of Hashem! It is impossible in any manner to explain to each person the straight path at every day and time — where to turn to save his soul from what he needs saving from. For <span class="v">"there is a path straight before a man, but its end is the ways of death."</span></div>

<div class="p">As our master, teacher, and rabbi <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> wrote on the verse <span class="v">"I wandered like a lost sheep"</span> etc. — as soon as one deviates slightly from the path, many confusing paths branch off from there, etc.; see there. Since most of the world has already wandered in the wilderness on a desolate path — for <span class="v">"there is no righteous person on earth"</span> etc., <span class="v">"who does not sin"</span> — through every sin, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, one strays from the path. As we confess every day: <span class="v">"we have gone astray, we have wandered"</span> etc. How is it possible to explain how to return to the straight path? Therefore the straight path cannot be explained explicitly at every moment and with every movement — only through allusion: <span class="v">"I shall enlighten you and guide you on this path you shall walk; I shall advise — My eye is upon you"</span> — one is hinted at every moment which way to go.</div>

<div class="p">All this is rectified through <span class="h">tzedakah</span>, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"the righteousness of the perfect shall straighten his way"</span>; and: <span class="v">"in the path of righteousness I walk."</span> Through <span class="h">tzedakah</span> one merits walking the straight path physically and spiritually, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"He guided them on a straight path"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the aspect of <span class="h">Remez</span> in Torah is the <span class="h">tikun</span> of journey-travelers, through <span class="h">tzedakah</span> that governs the celestial spheres from which all paths derive, as explained there; see there. The spheres revolve every day, every moment, every hour — from which all the changes in the world that Hashem orchestrates are drawn: heat and cold, summer and winter, etc. Also the particular changes every day, for every person, in every place. All in order to hint hints to a person to return to the straight path in truth, according to that day and hour, according to what is hinted at every moment. (As explained in the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">Vai'hi Mikeitz — Zikaron</span>," <span class="lr">LM I:54</span>: hints are given to a person at every moment to return to Hashem.) All through the changes made every day through the movement of the spheres, which the Holy One, blessed be He, governs with wondrous wisdom at every moment — <span class="v">"He changes the times and arranges the stars in their watches in the firmament according to His will"</span> etc. — all to hint to a person the straight path he must return to at that moment and hour.</div>

<div class="p">Those who damage this and do not take to heart all these hints that come through the movement of the spheres every day — about this it says: <span class="v">"they do not regard the work of Hashem and do not see the deeds of His hands."</span> As our Sages expound: they do not regard the work of Hashem to bless Him <span class="v">"who forms light"</span> in the morning and <span class="v">"who brings on evening"</span> in the evening — as above. For the essential blessing of <span class="v">"who forms light"</span> in the morning and <span class="v">"who brings on evening"</span> in the evening is to bless Him for the renewal of creation each day: <span class="v">"in His goodness He renews every day constantly the act of creation"</span> — all for the sake of the above hints, to return to the straight path and draw close to Him, as above, and as explained elsewhere. Thus <span class="h">Remez</span> is the aspect of the <span class="h">tikun</span> of journey-travelers, through <span class="h">tzedakah</span> governing the celestial spheres, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 6 — <i>Drush</i> = <i>Shabbos</i>-<i>Emunah</i> = Freedom from Prison</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Drush</span> [homiletical exposition] — this is the aspect of the <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">Shabbos</span>-<span class="h">emunah</span>, the world of freedom — the <span class="h">tikun</span> of one imprisoned who goes free. For the essential <span class="h">drushim</span> [homiletical expositions] of Torah are revealed on <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, as is known. All <span class="h">drushim</span> of Torah derive from the thirteen <span class="h">midos</span> through which the Torah is expounded — the aspect of the thirteen <span class="h">tikunai dikna</span> [rectifications of the "beard"], which are the light of the face. The essential illumination of the thirteen <span class="h">tikunai dikna</span> is on <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, when one ascends there, as known in the <span class="h">kavanos</span> of <span class="h">Shabbos</span>.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Shabbos</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">emunah</span> — through which specifically one can truly expound the Torah. <span class="h">Emunah</span> is the essential foundation upon which one stands — through which one enters the intellect to expound the Torah. As our master, teacher, and rabbi <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> wrote on the verse: <span class="v">"his hands were <i>emunah</i> until the sun came"</span> — through <span class="h">emunah</span> one comes to the intellect, etc.; see there.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore <span class="h">emunah</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, the world of freedom — through which comes the essential completeness of <span class="h">da'as</span> (as explained elsewhere, <span class="lr">LM II:10</span>), through which one can expound the Torah. For when <span class="h">emunah</span> is not in completeness, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, then one's <span class="h">da'as</span> and intellect are imprisoned and held in iron fetters, in captivity and prison, in great confinement.</div>

<div class="p">It is known that the human intellect is too short to grasp anything in its fullness and precision. Therefore all the philosophical scholars are confused and lost in all the wisdoms — they still have no clear wisdom, for there are among them many doubts, disputes, and tremendous confusions in all the wisdoms, as known to one versed in them. Through human intellect alone, it is impossible to grasp anything in its truth. Therefore, when one enters any wisdom without <span class="h">emunah</span>, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, his intellect is imprisoned and confined — unable to emerge from there. His mind remains confused and trapped in what he contemplates, unable to emerge in peace — for he cannot grasp it with his human intellect. As it says: <span class="v">"I said I would be wise, but it is far from me"</span>; <span class="v">"far is what was, and deep, deep — who can find it?"</span> (Koheles 7).</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the exile of Egypt, when <span class="h">da'as</span> was in exile and captivity, as is known. Egypt was full of idols — idolatry and heresies, the opposite of <span class="h">emunah</span>. Therefore <span class="h">da'as</span> was in exile and captivity there — for without <span class="h">emunah</span>, <span class="h">da'as</span> and intellect are imprisoned in exile and captivity, unable to escape the confusions through intellect alone without <span class="h">emunah</span>, as above. But when one has <span class="h">emunah</span>, the aspect of <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, he is in the aspect of freedom — for his mind is free. He knows everything on its basis through <span class="h">emunah</span> alone — by believing in the words of our Sages and holy forefathers. Then he has a foundation to stand upon — <span class="h">emunah</span>, upon which everything stands. Through the foundation of <span class="h">emunah</span>, he can enter all the <span class="h">drushim</span> of Torah and emerge in peace, since he has strong foundations through the holy <span class="h">emunah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of: <span class="mx">"Chavakuk came and established them on one: the righteous shall live by his faith."</span> <span class="v">"Shall live"</span> — this is the aspect of the true wisdoms of the <span class="h">drushim</span> of Torah, which are the essential vitality: <span class="v">"wisdom gives life"</span>; <span class="v">"seek Me and live"</span> — whose essence depends on <span class="h">emunah</span>, the foundation of the true <span class="h">drushim</span>, as above.</div>

<div class="p">This is what Rabbainu <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> writes in the Sefer HaAlef-Bais, under the letter <span class="h">peh</span>, "Redemption of Captives": "When one of Israel is imprisoned in confinement, through this — according to his level — the wellsprings of wisdom are blocked from the sages of the generation. And conversely, when he leaves prison [they are opened]." For imprisonment is the aspect of the wellsprings of wisdom being blocked — <span class="h">da'as</span> in exile and confinement, unable to sort the <span class="h">drushim</span> of Torah properly, as above. The <span class="h">tikun</span> of all this depends on <span class="h">Shabbos</span>-<span class="h">emunah</span>, the world of freedom, as above.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of: <span class="v">"I am Hashem your G-d who brought you out of the land of Egypt, from the house of bondage."</span> <span class="v">"I am Hashem your G-d"</span> — this is the aspect of <span class="h">emunah</span>, the essential foundation of <span class="h">emunah</span>: to believe in Him. Through this: <span class="v">"who brought you out of the land of Egypt, from the house of bondage"</span> — He brought us out of Egypt, from slavery, captivity, and imprisonment, all because <span class="h">da'as</span> was in exile there, as is known. Through <span class="h">emunah</span> — the aspect of <span class="v">"I am Hashem your G-d"</span> — <span class="h">da'as</span> leaves exile, captivity, and imprisonment: this is the essential aspect of <span class="h">Yetzias Mitzrayim</span>, as above. Thus <span class="h">Drush</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">Shabbos</span>-<span class="h">emunah</span>, the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the imprisoned who goes free, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 7 — <i>Sod</i> = <i>Bris Kodesh</i> = Sea-Travelers</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Sod</span> [the esoteric] — this is the aspect of the <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">bris kodesh</span>, the <span class="h">tikun</span> of sea-travelers, as above. For the essential secrets of Torah are merited through guarding the holy <span class="h">bris</span>, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"the secret of Hashem is for those who fear Him, and His covenant — to make it known to them."</span> <span class="h">Sod</span> has the numerical value of seventy — one merits this only through guarding the <span class="h">bris</span>, by breaking the desire that encompasses the evil of all the seventy nations, as explained in the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">B'krov Alai M'ra'im</span>" (<span class="lr">LM I:36</span>); see there, where he writes that by withstanding the trial of this desire — the essential trial and refining — the secrets of Torah are opened to him: <span class="mx">"hidden things are revealed to him"</span> etc.; see there.</div>

<div class="p">This is the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> of sea-travelers in ships, that they pass through in peace. The essential power to cross the sea derives from the aspect of: <span class="v">"who makes a way in the sea"</span> — from which the power to cross the sea in ships comes: <span class="v">"the way of a ship in the heart of the sea."</span> All this derives from <span class="h">tikun habris</span>, as the essential <span class="h">tikun habris</span> is in the aspect of <span class="v">"who makes a way in the sea,"</span> as explained (<span class="lr">LM II:87</span>) regarding the <span class="h">kavanos</span> of Elul, which are the <span class="h">tikun habris</span>; see there.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of attaining the secrets of Torah — about which it says spiritually: <span class="v">"those who go down to the sea in ships, doing work in great waters"</span> — only they descend into the sea of wisdom with great and wondrous effort, and only they see the works of Hashem and His awesome wonders: <span class="v">"they saw the works of Hashem and His wonders"</span> etc. No one knows and grasps the greatness of the Creator and His awesome wonders like the masters of the secrets of Torah, as brought in the holy Zohar and Tikunim. But no one merits this except the true guardians of <span class="h">bris kodesh</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore anyone wishing to enter <span class="h">PaRDeS</span> — the four parts of Torah: <span class="h">P'shat, Remez, Drush, Sod</span> — cannot enter and emerge in peace except by meriting all the above <span class="h">tikunim</span>. The essential thing depends on guarding <span class="h">bris kodesh</span>, as understood in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>. Then specifically one merits good longings and makes good souls and forms the letters of Torah for good — all four parts of Torah are formed and paired for good, to produce good actions in the world, as above. But one who, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, separates Torah-study from the above <span class="h">tikunim</span> — whose essence is the holiness of the <span class="h">bris</span> — and wants to enter <span class="h">PaRDeS</span> without those <span class="h">tikunim</span>, he can "cut the plantings," <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, like <span class="h">Acher</span>.</div>

<div class="p">The essential damage is when one wants to enter the secrets of Torah before having rectified <span class="h">bris kodesh</span> properly — for the essential attainment of the Torah's secrets is through guarding the <span class="h">bris</span>, as above. Even the other parts of Torah are not attained in completeness except through guarding the <span class="h">bris</span>. All the above <span class="h">tikunim</span> — <span class="h">emunah</span>, <span class="h">tzedakah</span>, etc. — all depend on guarding the <span class="h">bris</span>, as explained there. Through these <span class="h">tikunim</span> one merits all parts of Torah, as above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore every <span class="h">lamdan</span> must examine his deeds and look within himself: is he clean of sin? Is he holy in guarding the <span class="h">bris</span> properly? If he knows that he has not yet reached this — all the more if he is far from this <span class="h">tikun</span>, as far as is common now due to our many sins — he must know that his Torah still has no completeness except through the great <span class="h">tzadik</span> who is truly holy in the completeness of <span class="h">bris kodesh</span>. When one binds himself to him, then <span class="h">lamdan</span> and <span class="h">tzadik</span> are included together, and then he can be rectified through him — meriting good longings and forming the letters of Torah for good through him.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore every person must search and seek greatly for a true <span class="h">Rebbe</span> like this — which is the aspect of: <span class="mx">"if the teacher resembles an angel of Hashem Tz'vakos, Torah shall be sought from his mouth"</span> etc., as explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>. But if one does not draw close to such a true <span class="h">tzadik</span> — and all the more if he disputes him, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, while he himself is not holy at all, as he knows within himself, yet he prides and boasts only in his <span class="h">lamdan</span>-scholarship, since he is a great <span class="h">lamdan</span> — this is the aspect of <span class="h">Acher</span> who "cut the plantings." He separates <span class="h">lamdan</span> from <span class="h">tzadik</span>, thereby separating the letters of Torah from good vowel-points. The letters remain like a body without a soul — the aspect of <span class="mx">"if he did not merit, it becomes a potion of death."</span> This is literally the aspect of death — the departure of the soul from the body. For he too separates the good souls (= the vowel-points) from the letters (= the body relative to the vowel-points), as explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>; see there well.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 8 — The Damage of Korach: Separating <i>Lamdan</i> from <i>Tzadik</i></div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the damage of Korach, who disputed Moshe and Aharon. The aspect of: <span class="v">"Korach took"</span> — Targum: <span class="mx">"Korach separated"</span> — he separated <span class="h">lamdan</span> from <span class="h">tzadik</span> and said that being a <span class="h">lamdan</span> alone is a virtue. This is what he said: <span class="v">"the entire congregation, all of them are holy"</span> — Rashi: they all heard at Sinai "<span class="v">Anochi</span>" etc. He said: since they all received the Torah and all heard "<span class="h">Anochi</span>" at Sinai, therefore all faces are equal, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. In this he damaged and sinned greatly — for even though they all received the study of Torah at Sinai, they are not all equal in the fulfillment of Torah. The essential completeness is only according to the righteousness of each person, according to how each merits fulfilling the Torah in completeness. Everything depends on the holiness of the <span class="h">bris</span> — which no person knows about his fellow, only he knows his own deficiencies and how he stands in this.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one should certainly nullify himself before the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> — the aspect of Moshe — whose wondrous holiness is visible through the wondrous Torah he revealed to us, and rely on his words, that he certainly knows to whom to give greatness as befitting. Especially Moshe Rabbainu himself, who did everything according to the Divine command.</div>

<div class="p">Korach, through his arrogance, disputed him and boasted in his wisdom and learning, and derived a false <span class="h">kal vachomer</span>, and revealed faces in the Torah not according to <span class="h">halachah</span>. He said: a garment entirely of <span class="h">techailes</span> — is it exempt from a <span class="h">techailes</span> thread? etc. And a room full of <span class="h">sefarim</span> — does it require a <span class="h">mezuzah</span>? etc. When one has no <span class="h">emunah</span> and separates Torah-study from the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, he can certainly depart entirely from the faith, revealing faces in Torah not according to <span class="h">halachah</span> — like Korach who disputed Moshe through this, and <span class="h">Acher</span> who "cut the plantings" through this, as above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Korach disputed the <span class="h">kehunah</span> [priesthood] — for the <span class="h">Kohain</span> is the aspect of the above-mentioned true <span class="h">Rav</span>: <span class="v">"for the lips of the Kohain shall guard knowledge, and Torah..."</span>; <span class="mx">"if the teacher resembles an angel of Hashem Tz'vakos"</span> etc. <span class="h">Kohain</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">chesed</span>, the aspect of Avraham — love, yearning, and holy longings merited through this <span class="h">Rav</span> who resembles an angel of <span class="v">"Hashem Tz'vakos,"</span> as above. Korach, who damaged this and wanted to separate <span class="h">lamdan</span> from <span class="h">tzadik</span>, therefore disputed the <span class="h">kehunah</span>, as above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Hashem commanded sorting the truth through the staffs: <span class="v">"the man whom I choose — his staff shall blossom."</span> <span class="h">Mateh</span> [staff] is the aspect of <span class="h">Metat</span> — the aspect of a <span class="h">lamdan</span> encompassing the six orders of Mishnah, the aspect of forbidden and permitted, as explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>. Hashem commanded each one to place his staff before the Ark of Testimony — <span class="v">"the man whom I choose, his staff shall blossom"</span> — to demonstrate that Aharon's staff (= the true <span class="h">Rav</span>, the angel of <span class="v">"Hashem Tz'vakos"</span> who unites <span class="h">lamdan</span> and <span class="h">tzadik</span>) — he alone produces fruit: true Torah-novellae, which are the aspect of <span class="h">PaRDeS</span>. Only his staff blossomed and grew and produced fruit: <span class="v">"it brought forth a blossom and flowered a flower"</span> etc. — his Torah-study (= the <span class="h">mateh</span>) alone succeeded and bore fruit. For he is an angel of <span class="v">"Hashem Tz'vakos"</span> who binds <span class="h">lamdan</span> to <span class="h">tzadik</span> — through which the essential completeness of Torah-novellae (= <span class="h">PaRDeS</span>) is accomplished.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the <span class="h">kehunah</span> certainly belongs to him — for he merited holy longings and love, the aspect of <span class="h">Kohain</span>, man of <span class="h">chesed</span>, as above. But the staffs of the other princes did not blossom or produce fruit — demonstrating and revealing that they cannot innovate in Torah's <span class="h">PaRDeS</span> (called <span class="v">"a fruit tree producing fruit,"</span> as is known) except through the power of Aharon the <span class="h">Kohain</span>, the true <span class="h">Rav</span> resembling an angel of <span class="v">"Hashem Tz'vakos"</span> encompassing <span class="h">lamdan</span> and <span class="h">tzadik</span>. The <span class="h">kehunah</span> belongs to him alone.</div>

<div class="p">But one who separates himself from him is the aspect of a dry tree — even if it seems he innovates some novella, it is not considered fruit-bearing at all. As stated in the Zohar: novellae not as they should be are the aspect of <span class="mx">"false firmaments,"</span> <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> (as explained in the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">Vai'hi Mikeitz</span>," <span class="lr">LM I:54</span>) — the aspect of the seven years of famine, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, the opposite of fruit-bearing. It is impossible to succeed in Torah and truly bear fruit except by binding oneself to the true <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the aspect of Aharon the <span class="h">Kohain</span>, <span class="v">"the lips of the Kohain shall guard knowledge"</span> etc. — only he merited that his staff (= <span class="h">lamdan</span>, <span class="h">Metat</span>) blossoms and bears fruit. The essential completeness of Torah-novellae (= <span class="h">PaRDeS</span>, where all kinds of fruit grow) is only through good longings, through which one merits good deeds, as above.</div>

